# Nordshrift Munction Component Model — NS-MUN-0001

Status: implementation model
Revision: 1.0.0
Date: September 2026

## Purpose

Defines the Munction reach-composition components exposed to Nordshrift/Sleela
builds. Munction is a fluent reach sentence (4..16 verbs) over the known
system-method channels; the same reach series can be represented in Sleela
source, emitted as C-backed execution, or represented as structural metadata.

## Verbs (closed)

start, connect, open, enable, send, thatch, consume, observe, propagate,
latch, contain, close, closeWithReceipt, abort

## Channels (closed)

- `pipe`   — OS pipe / FIFO
- `file`   — file I/O
- `tcp`    — internet (TCP)
- `http`   — internet (HTTP)
- `sdps`   — private packets (Secure Datum Packet Stream: magic+length+MAC)
- `crypto` — authenticated seal/open envelope wrapping an inner channel

## Invariants

- Sanity bound: 4 <= verbs <= 16 (fixed by the language).
- Receivable: every reach yields a receipt.
- Coherent send: offered vs. acknowledged bytes are accounted; a bump is
  recorded, never hidden.

## Execution ABI

The core VM exposes Munction as VM opcodes with VM-local reach handles:
`OP_MUN_START`, `OP_MUN_CONNECT`, `OP_MUN_ENABLE`, `OP_MUN_SEND`,
`OP_MUN_THATCH`, `OP_MUN_CONSUME`, `OP_MUN_LATCH`, `OP_MUN_RECEPTION`,
`OP_MUN_CLOSE`. These back the `Munction.start(...)` fluent source surface.

## SST guidance

See `SST.MUNCTION.md` for the `reach:` block grammar and `examples/reach-measure.sst`.
